    "question": {
      ":question/id": "a858b1c1-cd18-59d7-9730-94cd391fddd6",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the numbers below belong to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$ is the set of all positive real numbers and zero.\n\n- $4.2=\\frac{21}{5}$: It is positive, and it is a rational number but not an integer, since it can be written as $\\frac{21}{5}$. Therefore, it belongs to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $-\\sqrt{49}=-7$: It is negative, and it is an integer. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $\\frac{13}{2}$: It is positive, and it is a rational number but not an integer. Therefore, it belongs to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $\\frac{35}{7}=5$: It is positive, and it is an integer. Therefore, it belongs to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is: $4.2$; $\\frac{13}{2}$; $\\frac{35}{7}$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
