    "question": {
      ":question/id": "a2532721-6865-50c0-a691-282c2e1d30d6",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the numbers below belong to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$ is the set of all positive rational numbers and zero.\n\n- $-\\frac{\\pi}{2}$: It is negative, and it is an irrational real number. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $-\\frac{26}{3}$: It is negative, and it is a rational number but not an integer. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $-\\frac{28}{8}=-\\frac{7}{2}$: It is negative, and it is a rational number but not an integer. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $\\frac{19}{5}$: It is positive, and it is a rational number but not an integer. Therefore, it belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is: $\\frac{19}{5}$.",
