      ":question/problem": "Which of the numbers below belong to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{-}$?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that $\\mathbb{R}_0^{-}$ is the set of all negative real numbers and zero.\n\n- $0$: Zero is included by the subscript $0$. Therefore, it belongs to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{-}$.\n\n- $-\\frac{10}{2}=-5$: It is negative, and it is an integer. Therefore, it belongs to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{-}$.\n\n- $\\frac{4}{9}$: It is positive, and it is a rational number but not an integer. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{-}$.\n\n- $\\pi$: It is positive, and it is an irrational real number. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{-}$.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is: $0$; $-\\frac{10}{2}$.",
